<p class="heritage-longread__dek">A companion to the Rycroft story. The Steinkes reach this family through <strong>Bette Doreen Steinke</strong>, who married Sam Rycroft in 1959. Until the evening of 10 June 2026 this page went no further back than Bette&rsquo;s parents &mdash; Henry and Martha &mdash; and named, as the work still to do, the chase that might one day carry the line across the ocean to the German village it came from. That evening the floor dropped two generations. The line now reaches, with high confidence, to <strong>Edward Steinke</strong> of Drasna, Volhynia (1873) and <strong>Augusta Woitt</strong> of East Prussia (1874), who married in Winnipeg on 27 November 1892 and crossed the continent at the head of a wave of Volhynian-German Lutheran settlers.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-durchhalten"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Prologue</span>Durchhalten</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">Volhynia · East Prussia · the Canadian plains</p><p>There is a German word for what carried families like the Steinkes: <em>durchhalten</em> &mdash; to hold out, to endure, to keep your grip on a thing through a long hard stretch when letting go would be easier. It is the word for a German farm family that pulled up its roots, crossed to the Canadian plains among strangers, and held a corner of cold prairie until it became home. The Steinkes did that. We now know the names of the places they held out from, and the names of the people who carried the holding forward.</p><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>Durchhalten &mdash; to hold out.</p></aside><p>What follows is the record of as much of them as the documents and the family tree have so far given up. What rests on primary record is told as fact; what rests on the family tree is named as such. The page will keep growing as the German parish registers and the older Canadian records release what they still hold.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-edward-steinke-and-augusta-woitt-out-of-volhynia"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter One</span>Edward Steinke and Augusta Woitt &mdash; out of Volhynia and East Prussia</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">Drasna, Volhynia 1873 · East Prussia 1874 · Winnipeg 1892</p><p>The deepest verified floor of the Steinke line stands on two people. <strong>Edward Steinke</strong> was born on 6 September 1873 in the German colony of <strong>Drasna, in Volhynia</strong> &mdash; the region of western Russian Ukraine where Lutheran farmers from East Prussia, Pomerania and central Poland had been settled across the nineteenth century to work the land. <strong>Augusta Woitt</strong> was born around 10 February 1874 in <strong>East Prussia</strong>. The family tree, sourced from the German-Lutheran parish databases that record this community, names Edward&rsquo;s parents as <strong>Michal Steinke and Eufrozyna Retz</strong>, and Augusta&rsquo;s as <strong>Gottlieb Woitt and Anna Rosalina Nast</strong> &mdash; couples whose names survive on the page even where their own primary-source records have not yet been pulled.</p><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>From that one entry the entire Canadian arc of the Steinke line proceeds.</p></aside><p>Edward came to Canada as a young man, almost certainly in 1891, entering through <strong>Emerson, Manitoba</strong> on the rail line up from St. Paul &mdash; the same crossing that admitted a whole generation of Volhynian and Galician Lutherans onto the plains. Augusta came in the same wave. On 27 November 1892 the two of them stood in front of a Winnipeg clerk and were married. The registration sits in the Manitoba Vital Statistics index as marriage number 1892,001370, and from that one entry the entire Canadian arc of the Steinke line proceeds.</p><p>He was not the only Steinke on that crossing. The 1931 Winnipeg census records a brother, <strong>August Steinke</strong>, born in Russia about 1888 and arriving in 1892, who stayed in the city and worked his life as a carpenter; a younger brother also named <strong>Henry</strong> was born in Manitoba in 1899, after the family arrived, and lived single his whole life. The Steinke parents therefore crossed too. The family tree records Edward&rsquo;s father, Michal, dying back at Alexandrov, Gostynin, in Russian Poland in 1908, and Augusta&rsquo;s parents, Gottlieb and Anna Rosalina Woitt, dying at Wetaskiwin, Alberta, in the early 1920s. The German records that confirm these lines are still being read.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-the-prairie-years-manitoba-to-wetaskiwin-to-the-"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Two</span>The prairie years &mdash; Manitoba to Wetaskiwin to the Peace, 1892 to 1928</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">Emerson · Wetaskiwin · the Peace · 1892 to 1928</p><p>Edward and Augusta began their married life in the Rural Municipality of Franklin, in southern Manitoba near the Emerson border. Their first children were born there. A son <strong>Albert Steinke</strong>, born at Emerson on 4 January 1899, is in the Manitoba vital index &mdash; primary confirmation of the family&rsquo;s southern-Manitoba years. Twelve more children would follow, across two more provinces.</p><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>Augusta bore those five graves and seven more living children, and held the line.</p></aside><p>By about 1905 the Steinkes had moved west to <strong>Wetaskiwin</strong>, south of Edmonton, where a Volhynian-German settlement had taken root. The 1911 census finds Edvard (in the enumerator&rsquo;s hand) and Augusta in Strathcona Sub-Districts 33 to 52 with seven children at home: Ludwig 17, Wilhelm 13, Albert 12, August 10, Augusta 5, Gustave 3, and <strong>Henry, age 2</strong> &mdash; born in Alberta, and the son through whom the line runs to Bette.</p><p>The full count of Edward and Augusta&rsquo;s children, as the cemetery memorial and the family tree carry it together, is fourteen: Ludwig (1893 to 1937), Wilhelm &ldquo;Bill&rdquo; (1897 to 1971), Albert (1899 to 1987), Helena (1899 to 1899), August (1901 to 1912), Bertha (1902 to 1903), Mathilde (1904 to 1906), Rudolf (1905 to 1906), Augusta (1906 to 1936), Gustave (1908 to 1975), <strong>Henry (1909 to 1984)</strong>, Frederick (1910 to 1990), Herman (1912 to 1969), and Martha Mae (1916 to 2008).</p><p>Five of those children &mdash; Helena, Bertha, Mathilde, Rudolf, and August &mdash; did not live to see the age of twelve. That is the rate of child mortality a Volhynian farm family was still meeting on the Canadian prairie in the first decade of the twentieth century, and it is part of the load that <em>durchhalten</em> carried. Augusta bore those five graves and seven more living children, and held the line.</p><p>By the late 1920s the family had moved one more time, north to the Peace Country, settling at <strong>Webster</strong>, the German-Lutheran district just west of Sexsmith. The 1931 census, taken at Grande Prairie sub-district on 1 June, finds Edward at the head of a household at section NW 9 of township 76, range 4, west of the sixth meridian, with Augusta and a clutch of the youngest children still at home &mdash; including Henry, then twenty-one and single, working alongside his father. A second Steinke household, almost certainly one of Edward&rsquo;s married sons, sat on the very next quarter section. The clan had pulled itself together again on the last farmable ground the prairies had to offer.</p><p>Edward died at Webster on 22 November 1940, aged sixty-seven. Augusta outlived him by twenty years and died at Sexsmith on 12 May 1961, aged eighty-seven. Both are buried at the <strong>Emmaus Lutheran Cemetery, Teepee Creek</strong> &mdash; the German Protestant burying-ground earlier drafts of this page had already named as the family&rsquo;s likely ground, before the records confirmed it.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-henry-steinke-1909-to-1984-the-son-who-carried-t"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Three</span>Henry Steinke (1909 to 1984) &mdash; the son who carried the line into the Peace</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">Alberta 1909 · Sexsmith 1932 · Grande Prairie 1984</p><p><strong>Henry Steinke</strong> was born on 20 April 1909 in Alberta &mdash; the eleventh of Edward and Augusta&rsquo;s fourteen children, and the first of them to be born after the move west of Manitoba. He spent his boyhood through the family&rsquo;s last migration, from Wetaskiwin up to the Peace, and his early manhood working the Webster farm. The 1926 prairie census places him at eighteen, still in his parents&rsquo; Alberta household; the 1931 census, at twenty-one, finds him there yet.</p><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>Henry and Martha had five children.</p></aside><p>In 1932, in the same Sexsmith district where the family had finally come to rest, he married <strong>Martha Mildred Gouchey</strong>, born 1911 &mdash; the woman whose maiden name this page had spent the most ink admitting it did not yet know. Together they raised <strong>five children</strong>. The two who can be named here are the eldest, <strong>Allan Henry Steinke</strong> (born at Sexsmith 31 July 1934, died 15 September 2020), whose own 2020 obituary names &ldquo;Henry and Martha Steinke&rdquo; as his parents; and <strong>Irvin Edward Steinke</strong> (1935 to 2011). The remaining three were daughters. The middle of those daughters was Bette.</p><p>Henry died on 24 May 1984, aged seventy-five, and was buried in Grande Prairie. Martha outlived him by fifteen years and died in 1999. The Find a Grave memorial that brought their two names together for this page closes with one quiet sentence &mdash; <em>Henry and Martha had five children</em> &mdash; and that, in the end, is the shape of their life together.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-bette-doreen-steinke-1942-to-2025-into-the-rycro"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Four</span>Bette Doreen Steinke (1942 to 2025) &mdash; into the Rycroft line</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">Sexsmith 1942 · married 1959 · Grande Prairie 2025</p><p><strong>Bette Doreen Steinke</strong> was born at Sexsmith on 26 April 1942 &mdash; the middle of Henry and Martha&rsquo;s three daughters, and the fourth of their five children. She came of age in the post-war Peace Country, and on 30 October 1959, at Sexsmith, she married <strong>Samuel Eric John &ldquo;Sam&rdquo; Rycroft</strong> &mdash; the grandson of a man born under a Hawaiian sky. A Volhynian-German Lutheran farmer&rsquo;s daughter and the descendant of a Honolulu soda-maker, married in a small prairie town: a meeting the Canadian west was built to make.</p><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>Not a name in an archive, but Nana Bette &mdash; remembered.</p></aside><div class="heritage-longread__ribbon" aria-hidden="true"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Edward &amp; Augusta</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">m. Winnipeg 1892</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Henry &amp; Martha</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">m. Sexsmith 1932</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Bette</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">1942-2025</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Lana</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">1961-2015</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Melanie</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">b. 1983</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Patience · Daniel · Faith</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">2013-2017</span></span></div><p>Sam and Bette raised four children &mdash; <strong>Lorne, Lana, Vance,</strong> and <strong>Clark</strong> &mdash; in the Grande Prairie country.</p><p>Bette outlived Sam by ten years. She died at Grande Prairie on 19 January 2025, aged eighty-two &mdash; recent enough that she is not a name in an archive but <strong>Nana Bette</strong>, remembered, photographed holding each of her great-grandchildren in turn.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-where-the-steinke-line-joins-the-others"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">The Living Line</span>Where the Steinke line joins the others</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">Grande Prairie · the confluence</p><p>The Steinke line is the youngest-known of the eight families that meet under the Cheesman name. It is now also one of the deepest-reached. It runs through <strong>Bette Doreen Steinke</strong> to her daughter <strong>Lana Lyn Rycroft</strong> (1961 to 2015), and through Lana to her daughter <strong>Melanie Lyn Haiste</strong> (born 1983), and through Melanie &mdash; with <strong>Thomas Cheesman</strong>, the co-parents of three children &mdash; to <strong>Patience, Daniel, and Faith</strong>, raised in Grande Prairie. They inherit, with everything else those eight lines pour into them, <em>durchhalten</em> &mdash; the holding-out that brought a Volhynian-German family across an ocean and a continent, held it together through five infant graves and three migrations, and handed three children in Grande Prairie a name and a grit they will carry forward whether or not they ever read the village it came from.</p><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>That is what durchhalten was for.</p></aside><p>That is what <em>durchhalten</em> was for.</p><details class="heritage-longread__notes" id="lr-notes">
<summary>Notes on the records</summary>
<div class="heritage-longread__notes-body">
<p>This page is no longer the thinnest-traced of the eight family lines. As of 10 June 2026 it rests on a combination of verified primary records, a record-derived cemetery memorial, and a well-sourced community family tree &mdash; and the tiers are named here so a careful reader knows which is which.</p><h3>Primary record &mdash; verified</h3><ul>
<li>Bette Doreen Steinke&rsquo;s parents named as Henry and Martha Steinke (Bear Creek Funeral Home obituary, 2025).</li>
<li>Allan Henry Steinke&rsquo;s parents named as Henry and Martha Steinke (Bear Creek Funeral Home obituary, 2020).</li>
<li>Edward Steinke × Augusta Woitt, married 27 November 1892, Winnipeg &mdash; Manitoba Vital Statistics marriage registration 1892,001370.</li>
<li>Albert Steinke, son of Edward and Augusta, born 4 January 1899, Emerson, Manitoba &mdash; Manitoba Vital Statistics birth registration 1899,22921154.</li>
<li>Edvard and Augusta Steinke, 1911 Census of Canada, Strathcona Sub-Districts 33 to 52, Alberta, with seven children at home including Henry, age 2.</li>
<li>August Steinke and household, 1931 Census of Canada, 676 Ross Avenue, Winnipeg North Centre 187, Manitoba &mdash; confirming a Steinke brother of Edward&rsquo;s generation in Winnipeg, immigrated 1892.</li>
</ul><h3>Memorial-derived &mdash; high confidence</h3><ul>
<li>Find a Grave memorial 137865383 for Henry Steinke (1909 to 1984), naming parents Edward Steinke and Augusta Woitt, his fourteen siblings, his wife Martha Mildred Gouchey (married 1932), and stating &ldquo;Henry and Martha had five children.&rdquo;</li>
<li>WikiTree profiles and Find a Grave memorials for Edward Steinke (born 6 September 1873, Drasna, Volhynia; died 22 November 1940, Webster) and Augusta Woitt Steinke (born around 10 February 1874, East Prussia; died 12 May 1961, Sexsmith), both buried Emmaus Lutheran Cemetery, Teepee Creek.</li>
</ul><h3>Family tree &mdash; to be verified</h3><ul>
<li>Edward&rsquo;s parents: Michal Steinke × Eufrozyna Retz.</li>
<li>Augusta&rsquo;s parents: Gottlieb Woitt × Anna Rosalina Nast &mdash; both said to have died at Wetaskiwin, Alberta, in the early 1920s.</li>
<li>The deeper generation, back to Jerzy Steinke × Doroty Schwartz, Jacob Retz × Maryanne Scheffler, Gottlieb Woitt × Anna Maria Schultz, and Andrzey Nast × Marianna Huff. These rest on community-curated Lutheran-parish databases for Volhynia and East Prussia; their primary records have not yet been pulled here.</li>
</ul><h3>Still to chase</h3><ul>
<li>Bette&rsquo;s 1959 Sexsmith marriage record &mdash; orderable now by a descendant through Alberta Vital Statistics, with proof of death and the relationship chain Lana to Melanie. Names Martha Gouchey&rsquo;s parents.</li>
<li>The 1931 census image for Edward&rsquo;s Grande Prairie sub-district household at NW 9-76-4-W6.</li>
<li>Martha Mildred Gouchey&rsquo;s own family records, parents and siblings &mdash; not yet on this page at all.</li>
<li>SGGEE (Society for German Genealogy in Eastern Europe) parish duplicates from the St. Petersburg Lutheran consistory, which would carry the Volhynian end of the line to baptism and burial registers.</li>
<li>A look at the Pomeranian / East-Prussian end through the Woitt and Nast surnames.</li>
</ul><p class="heritage-longread__aside"><em>This page now stands on what the records support, and names the rest as the work still to do.</em></p>
